From 8fee7b700e1cbed9008bcaeb48fbcdb787516119 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 09:00:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 40/41] Drone: life (drift, breath, shimmer), FM that blooms
 (sine or saw), Pulse timbre

The drone moves by itself like a played instrument, as chosen from the prototypes (render.py stage 4, render5.py a
and b). With life and FM at 0 and no Pulse tone it's v14's drone bit for bit: renderVoice() is kept as it was (the
firmware's float optimisations would round a version shared with the new code differently) and lively tones go to
renderLivelyVoice(). The song test's measured.wav is v14's (87a7df29).

- Life (0 to 50, drone-wide): each tone on its own slow random courses (white noise through two one-pole low-passes,
  a step per block, xorshift64, never repeating; both sides of a beating tone together, so the beat stays as set):
  pitch drift 6 cents standard deviation at 0.08 Hz, breath 3 dB at 0.12 Hz, shimmer: brightness (a crossfade
  towards the fundamental alone, up to 60 %) and pan +-0.25 at 0.05 Hz, all growing linearly with life. Rate (0 to
  50, 25 as designed): each 12.5 steps twice or half as fast.
- FM (0 to 50): a modulator at each side's frequency plus 0.3 Hz moves its phase, the index blooming as 2 rad x
  FM / 50 x clip(0.5 + 0.5 r)^2 on its own course (0.07 Hz, with the rate). Form Sine or Saw (8 harmonics, 1.3 times
  as deep), crossfading when switched. So high tones don't alias badly, the index is limited by a budget of index
  times frequency per timbre and form, and by the sidebands of the table's highest harmonic (for tones whose highest
  harmonic is just below 18 kHz): with the sine modulator nothing aliases above -50 dB, with the saw no more than in
  the chosen prototype (about -28 dB, from its reset's sweep). A DC blocker (two one-pole high-passes at 3 Hz) takes
  out the sideband 1:1 FM puts near 0 Hz, which ducking or an isochronic pulse would turn into thumps.
- Pulse timbre (4): the difference of two reads of new band-limited saw tables (24 harmonics, fewer for high tones),
  width drone-wide 5 to 50 % (default 30), wandering +-12 % x life / 50 at 0.1 Hz between 8 and 50 %.
- Changes of life, FM and its form glide or crossfade; to and from Pulse a tone goes through silence as before.
- Drone view: the Delay section (mod button 3) has Life (upper) and Rate, the ModFX section (5) FM (upper; pressed:
  Sine or Saw) and the pulse width, with popups and LED rings as the other sections. Menu: Life, Rate, FM, FM form,
  Pulse width; Timbre: Pulse.
- Song file: life, lifeRate, fm, fmForm and pulseWidth on the drone element, timbre 4. Without them a song loads the
  defaults; older firmware ignores them (a Pulse tone becomes Rich).
- The courses are seeded from jcong (only read) when the drone first sounds.
- Cost on the Cortex-A9 per binaural tone and 128 samples: 6.4k instructions off, 8.5k with life (sine), 12.1k with
  life (soft), 14.7k FM, 11.7k pulse, 24.5k all three. The song test's 4 tones: 20.7k -> 88.5k per 128 samples (1.8
  -> 7.6 % CPU). RAM: 14 KB more (the saw tables).

